WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.445
Upload authorization answers who is allowed to submit which file for which purpose.

2
00:00:05.445 --> 00:00:11.480
A user may be allowed to upload a profile photo but not attach documents to another account.

3
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:16.269
A partner may submit invoices for one tenant but not reports for another.

4
00:00:16.269 --> 00:00:22.305
The upload workflow should enforce purpose, ownership, tenant, and role rules on the server.

5
00:00:22.305 --> 00:00:26.372
Object-level authorization is just as important for downloads.

6
00:00:26.372 --> 00:00:30.505
A file has an owner, tenant, case, record, or workflow context.

7
00:00:30.505 --> 00:00:43.757
Before serving the file, the application should verify that the current user or service is allowed to access that specific object, not merely that they are logged in or have access to a general feature.

8
00:00:43.757 --> 00:00:47.627
Private files and public files need different access rules.

9
00:00:47.627 --> 00:00:54.844
A private support attachment should not become public because it shares a storage service with product images.

10
00:00:54.844 --> 00:01:05.471
A public product image may be safe to cache and serve broadly, while a private document may require authenticated access, audit records, and short-lived delivery.

11
00:01:05.471 --> 00:01:12.491
Avoid exposing internal storage paths, bucket paths, server file paths, or implementation details to users.

12
00:01:12.491 --> 00:01:20.626
Public identifiers, opaque file IDs, or controlled download routes are usually better than leaking how storage is organized.

13
00:01:20.626 --> 00:01:27.776
Internal paths can create coupling and reveal operational information even when the file itself is protected.

14
00:01:27.776 --> 00:01:31.844
Download authorization should happen before content is served.

15
00:01:31.844 --> 00:01:42.930
This applies to direct application responses, redirect-style delivery, temporary access links, object storage downloads, previews, thumbnails, and processed derivatives.

16
00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:47.916
Derivative files should not bypass the access rules of their source content.

17
00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:54.345
Temporary access links need expiration, revocation expectations, least privilege, and clear scope.

18
00:01:54.345 --> 00:02:03.792
Administrative and support access should receive extra scrutiny because those workflows often combine broad visibility with operational urgency.

19
00:02:03.792 --> 00:02:08.909
Secure downloads are part of the upload workflow, not a separate afterthought.
